WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.635
-Our next guest
is a talented actor

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:04.236
you know from such shows
as "Superstore"

00:00:04.236 --> 00:00:06.706
and the animated series
"Chicago Party Aunt."

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:08.675
He stars in "American Auto,"

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:10.944
which airs Tuesday nights
at 8:00 right here on NBC.

00:00:10.944 --> 00:00:12.445
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:14.914
-Cy-dawg! Heads up!

00:00:14.914 --> 00:00:18.517
-Oh, my God, Oh, my God!
I'm in hell!

00:00:18.517 --> 00:00:21.654
-You are officially invited
to join the pain fun patrol.

00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:23.322
-[ Spits ] It's in my mouth.
-We're going to go around.

00:00:23.322 --> 00:00:25.125
We're going to do fun things,
raise morale.

00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:26.392
It'll be great.
-I need an eye wash.

00:00:26.392 --> 00:00:27.794
Why did you have to do this?!

00:00:27.794 --> 00:00:29.328
-Because this is
my family's company,

00:00:29.328 --> 00:00:32.264
and if people aren't happy here,
then I'm going to fix it.

00:00:32.264 --> 00:00:34.801
Welcome to the team.

00:00:34.801 --> 00:00:36.302
Whoo!

00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:39.339
-Please welcome to the show
our friend Jon Barinholtz.

00:00:39.339 --> 00:00:41.007
Hey, Jonny!

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:44.176
-Heyyy! How are ya?

00:00:44.176 --> 00:00:47.547
-Now, Jon, you were
supposed to be here in person,

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:49.649
and I would be deeply offended
by the fact

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:52.051
that you backed out to do
this show from LA via Zoom,

00:00:52.051 --> 00:00:54.554
but you have
the best possible reason.

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:56.722
Four days ago, you had a baby.

00:00:56.722 --> 00:00:57.957
-Yes. We had a baby.

00:00:57.957 --> 00:01:02.061
We welcomed our second baby
into our family.

00:01:02.061 --> 00:01:05.565
Yeah, it's very crazy.
I'm on very little sleep.

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:08.468
As of a week ago, I was
still planning on coming out.

00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:10.836
We thought he was
going to be here early.

00:01:10.836 --> 00:01:14.006
And as of, like, last Monday,
my wife was like,

00:01:14.006 --> 00:01:16.642
"If he comes today or tomorrow,
I think you should still go.

00:01:16.642 --> 00:01:19.312
This is our second rodeo.
Our parents are here helping.

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:22.014
And you'll have seven days
under the belt."

00:01:22.014 --> 00:01:24.149
And then by Wednesday,
he still wasn't here,

00:01:24.149 --> 00:01:27.319
and it changed from her saying,
"I think you should go,"

00:01:27.319 --> 00:01:30.290
to, "I think you should still go
if you really want to."

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:32.825
-A very, very wise decision.

00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:36.362
Was the birth uneventful?
Healthy? Everything good?

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:37.830
-Healthy. Everyone's good.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.699
I was talking to my wife
about it.

00:01:39.699 --> 00:01:42.301
Seth, it's so silly
to come onto your show

00:01:42.301 --> 00:01:43.603
and tell a birth story.

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:44.970
You, like -- You're the GOAT.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:46.505
You jumped the shark
for birth stories

00:01:46.505 --> 00:01:47.907
for everyone
with the lobby birth.

00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:50.142
-Yep. Absolutely.
And I did all the work.

00:01:50.142 --> 00:01:53.346
-You did all the work.
It was all you. Congratulations.

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:55.114
No. Ours was very --
It was textbook.

00:01:55.114 --> 00:02:00.186
We were in at 11:00 a.m.,
and by dinnertime, he was here.

00:02:00.186 --> 00:02:04.123
There was one moment
that was pretty scary,

00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:06.492
and it speaks
to who I am as a person.

00:02:06.492 --> 00:02:11.363
At around, like, 5:30,
the doctor came and in was like,

00:02:11.363 --> 00:02:14.066
"I think you have a couple
of hours left of labor."

00:02:14.066 --> 00:02:15.502
We're like, "Great."

00:02:15.502 --> 00:02:18.738
So I said, "Annika, hey, these
nurses have been so great."

00:02:18.738 --> 00:02:22.242
Shout-out to Tarzana Providence.
Anabelle. She's the best.

00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:23.909
I was like,
"They've been so great.

00:02:23.909 --> 00:02:26.045
I'm going to order
a bunch of pizza for them.

00:02:26.045 --> 00:02:28.848
Also for me.
What do you think?"

00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:30.716
And she was, like,
on an epidural.

00:02:30.716 --> 00:02:33.319
She's like, "I don't care.
Go do whatever."

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:36.289
So I ordered it,
and about a half-hour later,

00:02:36.289 --> 00:02:38.257
the doctor comes back in
and checks Annika,

00:02:38.257 --> 00:02:40.693
and she takes out the stirrups
and puts them up.

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:41.994
I'm like, "What's happening?"

00:02:41.994 --> 00:02:44.030
The doctor's like,
"It's go time."

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.766
And right when she said that,
my phone dinged, and it said,

00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:50.236
"Andrew P is out
with your delivery order."

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:52.171
Right then,
I was like, "Oh, no."

00:02:52.171 --> 00:02:55.174
I saw these trains colliding,
and either, like,

00:02:55.174 --> 00:02:57.443
this has to go faster
or Andrew P

00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:59.378
has to get caught in traffic
because something's --

00:02:59.378 --> 00:03:00.980
I haven't seen the movie
"Perfect Storm,"

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:02.949
but I think
it's about this situation.

00:03:02.949 --> 00:03:05.418
And, I --

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:07.619
This is COVID times, too,
so you don't have friends

00:03:07.619 --> 00:03:10.089
who can go down to the lobby
and bring food up.

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:11.490
It's just us.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.525
So I pulled one of the nurses
aside, and I was like,

00:03:13.525 --> 00:03:16.462
"Hey, I ordered a bunch of food
for everyone.

00:03:16.462 --> 00:03:19.431
Is there someone who could
maybe run down and go grab it?"

00:03:19.431 --> 00:03:21.100
She was like, "What?

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.403
No, we're insanely understaffed.
Get away from me."

00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:25.805
I was like,
"Yeah, of course. Of course."

00:03:25.805 --> 00:03:29.375
So, you know, 15 minutes later,
now we're in full push mode,

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:32.545
baby is coming out,
and my phone dings again.

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:33.980
And I don't look at it, Seth,

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:37.316
because I'm trying to stay
present for the birth of my son.

00:03:37.316 --> 00:03:39.251
But at the same time,
I know what that ding means.

00:03:39.251 --> 00:03:41.254
I know it's Postmates saying,

00:03:41.254 --> 00:03:43.889
"Andrew P is arriving
with your order."

00:03:43.889 --> 00:03:47.627
And one final push,
and the baby's here,

00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:49.194
and he's beautiful
and he's healthy,

00:03:49.194 --> 00:03:51.697
and we put him on my wife's
chest and we're all crying.

00:03:51.697 --> 00:03:53.333
And my wife and I,
we look at him,

00:03:53.333 --> 00:03:55.000
and she looks at me
and she's like,

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.235
"I love you so much."

00:03:56.235 --> 00:03:58.204
And I'm like,
"I love you so much.

00:03:58.204 --> 00:04:00.039
I'm so sorry.
I have to go run down

00:04:00.039 --> 00:04:02.308
and get some pizza real quick."

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:04.644
And I did it.
I ran out of the room.

00:04:04.644 --> 00:04:06.112
I got the order.

00:04:06.112 --> 00:04:10.516
I said thank you to Andrew P,
because I'm a nice guy.

00:04:10.516 --> 00:04:12.118
I brought it back.
It was all good.

00:04:12.118 --> 00:04:14.754
They forgot one order of the
curly fries, but, you know...

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:18.024
-You know what? Let's not make
that what the day is about.

00:04:18.024 --> 00:04:19.791
-That was my lobby birth.
That was my lobby birth.

00:04:19.791 --> 00:04:23.862
-That's your lobby baby.
Hey, this show is a lot of fun.

00:04:23.862 --> 00:04:26.765
Ana Gasteyer. Great cast.

00:04:26.765 --> 00:04:30.603
We saw a little bit about --
So, your character --

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:34.306
you're the child of someone
who owns this auto company.

00:04:34.306 --> 00:04:36.375
It must be fun.

00:04:36.375 --> 00:04:38.711
-So much fun. It's another
Justin Spitzer show.

00:04:38.711 --> 00:04:40.213
So, I did "Superstore,"

00:04:40.213 --> 00:04:43.749
and that was a dream job
with an amazing cast.

00:04:43.749 --> 00:04:45.417
I feel like
I'm right back in it again.

00:04:45.417 --> 00:04:48.688
I feel so lucky to be
in another workplace comedy,

00:04:48.688 --> 00:04:51.590
another amazingly talented cast.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:53.325
And this is kind of the flip
of "Superstore."

00:04:53.325 --> 00:04:56.228
"Superstore" was, like,
the workers on the ground floor

00:04:56.228 --> 00:05:00.033
who are affected by, you know,
the bad decisions at corporate.

00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:01.734
This is now
the people of corporate

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:05.571
making those bad decisions,
and we see how they exist.

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:09.575
-I did not realize that
you had some automobile lineage

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:11.144
in your own family.

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:13.512
-I do. I do.

00:05:13.512 --> 00:05:16.815
On my mom's side,
we're a direct line

00:05:16.815 --> 00:05:19.986
of the Studebaker family
of Studebaker cars.

00:05:19.986 --> 00:05:23.022
And while that's like --
could be kind of cool,

00:05:23.022 --> 00:05:24.690
there are three
Studebaker brothers,

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:27.226
and our line
is Jacob Studebaker.

00:05:27.226 --> 00:05:31.263
And the brothers all got
together, you know, years ago.

00:05:31.263 --> 00:05:33.932
This is, I think,
great-great-great-grandfather.

00:05:33.932 --> 00:05:36.702
And they were like, "Hey, we're
going to start a car company."

00:05:36.702 --> 00:05:38.370
And two of the brothers
were like, "Great."

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:39.739
And Jacob was like,

00:05:39.739 --> 00:05:41.407
"I don't think cars
are gonna be a thing.

00:05:41.407 --> 00:05:42.875
I'm gonna stick with farming."

00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:44.209
[ Laughter ]

00:05:44.209 --> 00:05:46.111
So he continued on to Ohio

00:05:46.111 --> 00:05:47.846
and stayed in Ohio
and just became a farmer,

00:05:47.846 --> 00:05:50.750
and our family was farmers,
and then the other brothers went

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:52.485
and had a giant,
successful car company.

00:05:52.485 --> 00:05:54.720
[ Laughter ]

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:56.222
-We've known each other forever.

00:05:56.222 --> 00:05:58.457
I've known you
since you were a kid.

00:05:58.457 --> 00:06:00.526
Your brother, your older
brother, Ike Barinholtz,

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:02.928
a former roommate of mine,
a dear friend of mine.

00:06:02.928 --> 00:06:06.498
How is Ike as an older brother?
How would you define it?

00:06:06.498 --> 00:06:09.101
-Honestly, the best.
It's always been really great.

00:06:09.101 --> 00:06:11.671
You know, he's six years older,
and I want that on the record.

00:06:11.671 --> 00:06:14.639
I want everyone to know that.
Six years older.

00:06:14.639 --> 00:06:16.709
-He looks 50 years older.

00:06:16.709 --> 00:06:20.346
[ Laughter ]

00:06:20.346 --> 00:06:24.783
-I can't wait
for him to see that.

00:06:24.783 --> 00:06:26.552
He was very supportive of me.

00:06:26.552 --> 00:06:28.620
Lie, by the end
of my college career,

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:31.991
I knew I was gonna --
I wanted to go back to Chicago

00:06:31.991 --> 00:06:36.162
and do, like, comedy college,
so to speak, at Second City

00:06:36.162 --> 00:06:38.998
and Improv Olympic
and Annoyance.

00:06:38.998 --> 00:06:42.167
And I called him my junior year,
and I was like,

00:06:42.167 --> 00:06:44.103
"Hey, I know what I want to do.

00:06:44.103 --> 00:06:46.238
I'm done.
I want to go and do improv."

00:06:46.238 --> 00:06:49.374
And he right away was like,
"Don't be an idiot.

00:06:49.374 --> 00:06:50.475
You're a year away
from getting your degree.

00:06:50.475 --> 00:06:52.211
Get your degree

00:06:52.211 --> 00:06:55.247
just on the off chance that
improv comedy doesn't pan out."

00:06:55.247 --> 00:06:57.583
He was like,
"Get your college degree."

00:06:57.583 --> 00:07:00.352
So I'm very grateful that
I stuck around and did that.

00:07:00.352 --> 00:07:02.354
I learned a lot that last year.

00:07:02.354 --> 00:07:04.523
Yeah, but he's the best.

00:07:04.523 --> 00:07:09.061
-Well, you come from a very
good family of Ohio farmers,

00:07:09.061 --> 00:07:11.163
and it is wonderful
to have you here.

00:07:11.163 --> 00:07:13.299
Congratulations
on your new addition.

00:07:13.299 --> 00:07:14.734
Great to see you, Jonny.
-Thank you.

00:07:14.734 --> 00:07:16.302
-Jon Barinholtz, everybody.

00:07:16.302 --> 00:07:18.404
"American Auto" airs
Tuesday nights here on NBC.

00:07:18.404 --> 00:07:20.373
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

